    "thm-strongly-convex-regularized-erm-stability": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-uniform-algorithmic-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal Z$ be an observation set, let $\\mathcal H$ be a set of hypotheses, and let $L:\\mathcal H\\times\\mathcal Z\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a loss. A deterministic size-indexed learning rule consists, for every integer $n\\ge1$ and every retained index set $J\\subseteq[n]$, of a map $A_{n,J}$ from the indexed observations $(z_j)_{j\\in J}$ to $\\mathcal H$; the original sample size $n$ remains an input to this rule. For $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)$ write $A_S:=A_{n,[n]}(S)$ and $A_{S\\setminus i}:=A_{n,[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}}(S|_{[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}})$ for the output after deleting indexed observation $i$. The rule is permutation-invariant if simultaneous reordering of the observations and relabeling of their retained indices leaves each output unchanged. Given a nonnegative sequence $(\\beta_n)_{n\\ge1}$, the rule has uniform deletion stability with coefficients $(\\beta_n)$ if, for every $n\\ge1$, every $S\\in\\mathcal Z^n$, every $i\\in[n]$, and every $z\\in\\mathcal Z$,\n\n$$\n|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S\\setminus i},z)|\\le\\beta_n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence is nonincreasing when $\\beta_{n+1}\\le\\beta_n$ for every $n\\ge1$. This is the deletion convention. If $S'$ differs from $S$ only at coordinate $i$, then $S\\setminus i=S'\\setminus i$, and the triangle inequality gives $|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S'},z)|\\le2\\beta_n$ for every $z$; the resulting replacement bound has coefficient $2\\beta_n$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For vectors $u,v$ in a real or complex inner product space,\n\n$$|\\langle u,v\\rangle|\\le\\lVert u\\rVert\\lVert v\\rVert.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if $u$ and $v$ are linearly dependent, including the case in which either vector is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Write $F(f)=m^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^m c(f(x_j),y_j)+\\lambda\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal H}^2$ and $G(f)=m^{-1}\\sum_{j\\ne i}c(f(x_j),y_j)+\\lambda\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal H}^2$, with minimizers $f=A_S$ and $g=A_{S\\setminus i}$. For any convex $Q$ and $J_Q(h)=Q(h)+\\lambda\\|h\\|^2$, the identity $\\|(1-t)u+tv\\|^2=(1-t)\\|u\\|^2+t\\|v\\|^2-t(1-t)\\|u-v\\|^2$ and convexity of $Q$ give $J_Q((1-t)u+tv)\\le(1-t)J_Q(u)+tJ_Q(v)-\\lambda t(1-t)\\|u-v\\|^2$. If $u$ minimizes $J_Q$, then $J_Q(u)\\le J_Q((1-t)u+tv)$; subtracting $(1-t)J_Q(u)$, dividing by $t>0$, and letting $t\\downarrow0$ yields $J_Q(v)-J_Q(u)\\ge\\lambda\\|u-v\\|^2$. Applying this to every retained-set objective shows that each minimizer is unique. Each objective is unchanged by simultaneous reordering of the observations and relabeling of the retained indices, so the family is permutation-invariant. Apply the same gap inequality to $F$ at $f$ with $v=g$ and to $G$ at $g$ with $v=f$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Adding the two inequalities from step 1.1 cancels all common loss terms and both regularizers except the deleted observation, so $2\\lambda\\|f-g\\|_{\\mathcal H}^2\\le F(g)-F(f)+G(f)-G(g)=m^{-1}[c(g(x_i),y_i)-c(f(x_i),y_i)]\\le\\sigma|g(x_i)-f(x_i)|/m$. The RKHS identity in the Statement and [F2] give $|g(x_i)-f(x_i)|=|\\langle g-f,K_{x_i}\\rangle|\\le\\kappa\\|g-f\\|_{\\mathcal H}$. If $f=g$ the desired norm bound is immediate; otherwise dividing by $2\\lambda\\|f-g\\|_{\\mathcal H}$ yields $\\|f-g\\|_{\\mathcal H}\\le\\sigma\\kappa/(2\\lambda m)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "For any test pair $(x,y)$, $\\sigma$-Lipschitzness and the reproducing-kernel estimate give $|c(f(x),y)-c(g(x),y)|\\le\\sigma|\\langle f-g,K_x\\rangle|\\le\\sigma\\kappa\\|f-g\\|_{\\mathcal H}\\le\\sigma^2\\kappa^2/(2\\lambda m)$. Taking the supremum over samples, deleted indices, and test pairs gives the stated stability coefficient. Since $1/m$ decreases with $m$ for fixed positive $\\lambda$, the displayed envelope is nonincreasing.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
