      "finite_checks": [],
      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-6",
        "notes": "Read Statement and Proof 1.1\u20133.1, deletion Definition and full Cauchy\u2013Schwarz supplier. For 0<t<1 norm interpolation and convexity give the gap lambda||u-v||\u00b2 by t down to zero. Attainment for every retained set gives uniqueness and permutation invariance. Two gap inequalities cancel common terms, leaving one loss divided by original m; Lipschitz and bounded evaluation give sigma\u00b2 kappa\u00b2/(2 lambda m). Zero sigma/kappa and coincident minimizers avoid division by zero; m=1 empty objective has unique zero minimizer. No AC, bounded-loss conclusion or existence proof assumed. Both undefined J occurrences corrected to J_Q. Bousquet\u2013Elisseeff Theorem 22 proof pp.514\u2013515 matches; reader full-family repair retained."
      }
    },
    "thm-uniform-stability-expected-generalization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-supervised-learning-problem",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **supervised prediction problem** consists of nonempty measurable spaces $(\\mathsf X,\\Sigma_X)$ of instances, $(\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_Y)$ of labels, and $(\\mathsf A,\\Sigma_A)$ of prediction actions; the product measurable space $(\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_X\\otimes\\Sigma_Y)$; a probability measure $P$ on that product; a nonempty class $\\mathcal F$ of measurable predictors $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$; and a loss $\\ell:\\mathsf A\\times\\mathsf Y\\to[0,+\\infty]$ that is measurable with respect to $\\Sigma_A\\otimes\\Sigma_Y$. The law $P$ need not be supported on a deterministic labeling function, and this definition imposes no assumption that sample observations be independent or share a common law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a supervised prediction problem with joint law $P$, the **population risk** of any measurable predictor $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$ (in particular, every $f\\in\\mathcal F$) is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(f):=\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}\\ell(f(x),y)\\,P(d(x,y))\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor an ordered sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_m,y_m))$ of length $m\\ge1$, the **empirical risk** or **training risk** of any such $f$ is\n$$\\widehat R_S(f):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(f(x_i),y_i)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nNeither definition assumes finite risk. The sum uses extended nonnegative arithmetic; since $m\\ge1$, any summand equal to $+\\infty$ makes the empirical risk $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B)$ be measurable spaces. Their\n**product sigma-algebra** is\n\n$$\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B := \\sigma(\\{A \\times B : A \\in \\mathcal A,\\ B \\in \\mathcal B\\}),$$\n\nthe sigma-algebra on $X \\times Y$ generated by the measurable rectangles of\n[[def-measurable-rectangle]].\n\nFor the empty family, put $\\prod_{i<0}X_i:=\\{()\\}$ and define its **empty\nproduct sigma-algebra** by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<0}\\mathcal A_i:=\\{\\varnothing,\\{()\\}\\}.$$\n\nFor a nonempty finite family of measurable spaces $(X_i,\\mathcal A_i)$ with\n$i<n$, the **finite product sigma-algebra** is defined recursively by\n\n$$\\bigotimes_{i<1}\\mathcal A_i := \\mathcal A_0,\\qquad \\bigotimes_{i<n+1}\\mathcal A_i := \\left(\\bigotimes_{i<n}\\mathcal A_i\\right)\\otimes\\mathcal A_n.$$\n\nThis recursive definition is the finite base case used later when the library\npasses to countable product constructions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-sigma-finite-product-measure-exists-is-rectangular-and-is-unique",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces. Then:\n\n1. the set function $E \\mapsto (\\mu \\times \\nu)(E)$ of\n   [[def-product-measure-on-sigma-finite-spaces]] is a measure on\n   $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$;\n2. for measurable rectangles,\n   $$(\\mu \\times \\nu)(A \\times B)=\\mu(A)\\nu(B),$$\n   with the nonnegative extended-product convention $0\\cdot\\infty=\\infty\\cdot0=0$;\n3. the measure $\\mu \\times \\nu$ is sigma-finite; and\n4. it is the unique measure on $\\mathcal A \\otimes \\mathcal B$ with the\n   rectangle formula.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ and $(Y,\\mathcal B,\\nu)$ be sigma-finite measure\nspaces, and let $f : X \\times Y \\to \\mathbb C$ belong to\n$L^1(\\mu \\times \\nu)$. Then:\n\n1. for $\\mu$-almost every $x$, the section $f_x$ belongs to $L^1(\\nu)$;\n2. for $\\nu$-almost every $y$, the section $f^y$ belongs to $L^1(\\mu)$;\n3. after assigning the value $0$ on the exceptional parameter sets, the\n   section-integral functions are integrable; and\n4. the three integrals agree:\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\times \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f_x\\,d\\nu\\right)d\\mu = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f^y\\,d\\mu\\right)d\\nu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-integrable-real-and-complex-functions-and-their-integrals",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be measurable. Its positive and negative parts are\n$$f^+:=\\max\\{f,0\\},\\qquad f^-:=\\max\\{-f,0\\};$$\nthey are measurable by\n[[prop-closure-properties-of-measurable-functions-used-by-the-integral]] and\nsatisfy $f=f^+-f^-$ and $|f|=f^++f^-$.\n\nThe **Lebesgue integral of a real measurable function** is defined whenever at\nmost one of $\\int f^+\\,d\\mu$ and $\\int f^-\\,d\\mu$ is $+\\infty$, in which case\n$$\\int f\\,d\\mu:=\\int f^+\\,d\\mu-\\int f^-\\,d\\mu.$$\nThe function is **integrable** when both integrals are finite, equivalently\nwhen $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$.\n\nFor a complex measurable function $h=u+iv$ with\n$u=\\operatorname{Re}h,\\ v=\\operatorname{Im}h$\n([[def-complex-conjugate-real-imaginary-part-and-modulus]]), define $h$ to be\n**integrable** when $|h|$ is integrable, and then define\n$$\\int h\\,d\\mu:=\\int u\\,d\\mu+i\\int v\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-linearity-of-the-lebesgue-integral-on-l-one",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The class $L^1(\\mu)$ is a complex vector space, and the Lebesgue integral is\ncomplex-linear on it:\n$$\\int (\\alpha f+\\beta g)\\,d\\mu=\\alpha\\int f\\,d\\mu+\\beta\\int g\\,d\\mu \\qquad(\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb C,\\ f,g\\in L^1(\\mu)).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-integral-triangle-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f\\in L^1(\\mu)$, then\n$$\\left|\\int f\\,d\\mu\\right|\\le\\int |f|\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-uniform-algorithmic-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal Z$ be an observation set, let $\\mathcal H$ be a set of hypotheses, and let $L:\\mathcal H\\times\\mathcal Z\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a loss. A deterministic size-indexed learning rule consists, for every integer $n\\ge1$ and every retained index set $J\\subseteq[n]$, of a map $A_{n,J}$ from the indexed observations $(z_j)_{j\\in J}$ to $\\mathcal H$; the original sample size $n$ remains an input to this rule. For $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)$ write $A_S:=A_{n,[n]}(S)$ and $A_{S\\setminus i}:=A_{n,[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}}(S|_{[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}})$ for the output after deleting indexed observation $i$. The rule is permutation-invariant if simultaneous reordering of the observations and relabeling of their retained indices leaves each output unchanged. Given a nonnegative sequence $(\\beta_n)_{n\\ge1}$, the rule has uniform deletion stability with coefficients $(\\beta_n)$ if, for every $n\\ge1$, every $S\\in\\mathcal Z^n$, every $i\\in[n]$, and every $z\\in\\mathcal Z$,\n\n$$\n|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S\\setminus i},z)|\\le\\beta_n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence is nonincreasing when $\\beta_{n+1}\\le\\beta_n$ for every $n\\ge1$. This is the deletion convention. If $S'$ differs from $S$ only at coordinate $i$, then $S\\setminus i=S'\\setminus i$, and the triangle inequality gives $|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S'},z)|\\le2\\beta_n$ for every $z$; the resulting replacement bound has coefficient $2\\beta_n$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Fix $i\\in[m]$ and extend the coordinate sample to $(\\mathsf Z^{m+1},P^{m+1})$, writing $Z_i'$ for the last coordinate. By [A1] and [F6], $(S,z')\\mapsto L_{A_S}(z')$ is integrable on this product space, and [F5] identifies $\\mathbb E_S R_P(A_S)$ with $\\mathbb E_{S,Z_i'}L_{A_S}(Z_i')$. On each all-coordinate rectangle, the $P^{m+1}$ mass is the product of its coordinate masses. Transposing coordinates $i$ and $m+1$ preserves that value; the generating and uniqueness statements in [F4] therefore show that the transposed product law is again $P^{m+1}$. If $S^{(i)}$ replaces $Z_i$ by $Z_i'$, this gives $\\mathbb E_{S,Z_i'}L_{A_S}(Z_i')=\\mathbb E_{S,Z_i'}L_{A_{S^{(i)}}}(Z_i)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "By [F2] and [F7], $R_P(A_S)-R_P(A_{S\\setminus i})=\\int_{\\mathsf Z}(L_{A_S}(z)-L_{A_{S\\setminus i}}(z))\\,P(dz)$. The integrand has absolute value at most $\\beta_m$ by [F9], so [F8] gives $|R_P(A_S)-R_P(A_{S\\setminus i})|\\le\\int\\beta_m\\,dP=\\beta_m$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "For each $i$, the deleted output $A_{S\\setminus i}$ depends only on the retained coordinates. Since $Z_i$ is independent of them and has law $P$, [F5] and [F6] give $\\mathbb E_S L_{A_{S\\setminus i}}(Z_i)=\\mathbb E_S R_P(A_{S\\setminus i})$. Averaging over $i$ therefore yields $\\mathbb E_S[R_P(A_S)-R_{\\mathrm{loo}}(A,S)]=m^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbb E_S[R_P(A_S)-R_P(A_{S\\setminus i})]$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "By [F2] and linearity, step 1.1 gives $\\mathbb E_S[R_P(A_S)-\\widehat R_S(A_S)]=m^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\mathbb E_{S,Z_i'}[L_{A_{S^{(i)}}}(Z_i)-L_{A_S}(Z_i)]$. The indexed retained samples $S\\setminus i$ and $S^{(i)}\\setminus i$ coincide. Applying [F9] to each full output and that common deletion output, then the triangle inequality, bounds the absolute value of every summand by $2\\beta_m$. Averaging gives the first expected bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F9",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Each summand in the average in step 1.3 is at most $\\beta_m$ by step 1.2, so its average is at most $\\beta_m$. This proves the leave-one-out bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "step 1.3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
