    "thm-uniform-stability-high-probability-generalization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a supervised prediction problem with joint law $P$, the **population risk** of any measurable predictor $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$ (in particular, every $f\\in\\mathcal F$) is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(f):=\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}\\ell(f(x),y)\\,P(d(x,y))\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor an ordered sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_m,y_m))$ of length $m\\ge1$, the **empirical risk** or **training risk** of any such $f$ is\n$$\\widehat R_S(f):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(f(x_i),y_i)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nNeither definition assumes finite risk. The sum uses extended nonnegative arithmetic; since $m\\ge1$, any summand equal to $+\\infty$ makes the empirical risk $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-uniform-algorithmic-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal Z$ be an observation set, let $\\mathcal H$ be a set of hypotheses, and let $L:\\mathcal H\\times\\mathcal Z\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a loss. A deterministic size-indexed learning rule consists, for every integer $n\\ge1$ and every retained index set $J\\subseteq[n]$, of a map $A_{n,J}$ from the indexed observations $(z_j)_{j\\in J}$ to $\\mathcal H$; the original sample size $n$ remains an input to this rule. For $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)$ write $A_S:=A_{n,[n]}(S)$ and $A_{S\\setminus i}:=A_{n,[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}}(S|_{[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}})$ for the output after deleting indexed observation $i$. The rule is permutation-invariant if simultaneous reordering of the observations and relabeling of their retained indices leaves each output unchanged. Given a nonnegative sequence $(\\beta_n)_{n\\ge1}$, the rule has uniform deletion stability with coefficients $(\\beta_n)$ if, for every $n\\ge1$, every $S\\in\\mathcal Z^n$, every $i\\in[n]$, and every $z\\in\\mathcal Z$,\n\n$$\n|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S\\setminus i},z)|\\le\\beta_n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence is nonincreasing when $\\beta_{n+1}\\le\\beta_n$ for every $n\\ge1$. This is the deletion convention. If $S'$ differs from $S$ only at coordinate $i$, then $S\\setminus i=S'\\setminus i$, and the triangle inequality gives $|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S'},z)|\\le2\\beta_n$ for every $z$; the resulting replacement bound has coefficient $2\\beta_n$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-uniform-stability-expected-generalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathsf X,\\Sigma_X)$ and $(\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_Y)$ be nonempty measurable instance and label spaces, and equip $\\mathsf Z=\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y$ with $\\Sigma_Z=\\Sigma_X\\otimes\\Sigma_Y$. Let $P$ be a probability measure on $(\\mathsf Z,\\Sigma_Z)$, let $\\mathcal F$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $f:(\\mathsf X,\\Sigma_X)\\to(\\mathbb R,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R))$, and let $\\ell:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathsf Y\\to[0,\\infty)$ be measurable with respect to $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)\\otimes\\Sigma_Y$. For $f\\in\\mathcal F$ and $z=(x,y)$ write $L_f(z)=\\ell(f(x),y)$, $R_P(f)=\\int_{\\mathsf Z}L_f(z)\\,P(dz)$, and $\\widehat R_S(f)=m^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^mL_f(Z_i)$. Let $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ have product law $P^m$ on $(\\mathsf Z^m,\\Sigma_Z^{\\otimes m})$, with $m\\ge1$, and let $A$ be a deterministic, permutation-invariant size-indexed learning rule that outputs members of $\\mathcal F$ and assume the maps $(S,z)\\mapsto L_{A_S}(z)$ and $(S,z)\\mapsto L_{A_{S\\setminus i}}(z)$ for every $i\\in[m]$ are jointly measurable with respect to $\\Sigma_Z^{\\otimes m}\\otimes\\Sigma_Z$. Assume also that the sample-dependent risk, empirical-risk, and leave-one-out maps in the conclusions are measurable. Assume $0\\le L_{A_T}(z)\\le M<\\infty$ for every full or deleted sample $T$ and every $z\\in\\mathsf Z$, and assume $A$ has uniform deletion stability with a finite nonincreasing sequence $(\\beta_n)_{n\\ge1}$. Define $R_{\\mathrm{loo}}(A,S)=m^{-1}\\sum_{i=1}^m L_{A_{S\\setminus i}}(Z_i)$, using the size-$m$ deletion output. Then\n\n$$\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\bigl[R_P(A_S)-\\widehat R_S(A_S)\\bigr]\\le2\\beta_m\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\bigl[R_P(A_S)-R_{\\mathrm{loo}}(A,S)\\bigr]\\le\\beta_m.$$\n\nMoreover, for every sample $S$ and index $i\\in[m]$,\n\n$$|R_P(A_S)-R_P(A_{S\\setminus i})|\\le\\beta_m.$$\n\nAll three conclusions are choice-free.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-mcdiarmid-bounded-differences",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $n\\ge1$, let $Z_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$ be independent random elements, meaning measurable maps ([[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]]), with laws $\\mu_i$ given by pushforward ([[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]). Put $\\mu=\\bigotimes_{i=1}^n\\mu_i$ on the finite product sigma-algebra ([[def-product-sigma-algebra-and-finite-product-sigma-algebras]]). Let $f:\\prod_{i=1}^nS_i\\to\\mathbb R$ be product-measurable with $\\int|f|\\,d\\mu<\\infty$. Suppose finite deterministic constants $c_i\\ge0$ satisfy\n$$|f(z_1,\\ldots,z_i,\\ldots,z_n)-f(z_1,\\ldots,z_i',\\ldots,z_n)|\\le c_i$$\nfor every fixed setting of the other coordinates and every $z_i,z_i'$. The independent product-law theorem identifies the law of $Z=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)$ as $\\mu$. Applying change of variables for expectation first to $|f|$ and then to $f$ makes $f(Z)$ integrable and gives $\\mathbb E f(Z)=\\int f\\,d\\mu$. Put $V=\\sum_{i=1}^n c_i^2$. For $t>0$, if $V>0$ then\n$$\\mathbb P\\bigl(f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)-\\mathbb E f(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_n)\\ge t\\bigr)\\le\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{2t^2}{V}\\right),$$\nand if $V=0$ this probability is $0$. The same bound holds for the lower tail, so the two-sided probability is at most $2e^{-2t^2/V}$ when $V>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Fix an index $k$ and let $S'$ be any sample that differs from $S$ only at coordinate $k$. Their retained samples $S\\setminus k$ and $S'\\setminus k$ coincide. By [F4], $|R_P(A_S)-R_P(A_{S'})|\\le|R_P(A_S)-R_P(A_{S\\setminus k})|+|R_P(A_{S'\\setminus k})-R_P(A_{S'})|\\le2\\beta_m$. For every $j\\ne k$, the two loss terms at the common test point $Z_j$ differ by at most $2\\beta_m$, by [F2] through the same deleted output. The two $k$th loss terms each lie in $[0,M]$, so their difference is at most $M$. Hence $|\\widehat R_S(A_S)-\\widehat R_{S'}(A_{S'})|\\le 2(m-1)\\beta_m/m+M/m\\le2\\beta_m+M/m$. For $\\Phi(S)=R_P(A_S)-\\widehat R_S(A_S)$ this yields the coordinate oscillation $|\\Phi(S)-\\Phi(S')|\\le4\\beta_m+M/m$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Fix an index $k$ and samples $S,S'$ differing only at coordinate $k$. The risk term in $\\Psi(S)=R_P(A_S)-R_{\\mathrm{loo}}(A,S)$ changes by at most $2\\beta_m$ by [F4]. In the leave-one-out average, the $k$th term uses the same deleted training sample for $S$ and $S'$, and its test losses differ by at most $M$. For $j\\ne k$, the two deleted training samples $S\\setminus j$ and $S'\\setminus j$ have the same $m-2$ common observations and the two alternative $k$th observations. Form a size-$m$ indexed sample $T$ from those $m-2$ common observations and both alternatives. Up to permutation, each of the two deleted training samples is obtained by deleting one of the alternatives from $T$. Permutation invariance and [F2] therefore bound their loss change at the common test point $Z_j$ by $2\\beta_m$. When $m=1$ there are no indices $j\\ne k$, so only the $k$th term occurs. Thus the leave-one-out average changes by at most $2(m-1)\\beta_m/m+M/m\\le2\\beta_m+M/m$, and $|\\Psi(S)-\\Psi(S')|\\le4\\beta_m+M/m$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Put $c=4\\beta_m+M/m$. If $c=0$, then $M=\\beta_m=0$ and every loss and both gaps vanish. If $c>0$, then $\\Phi$ is bounded, measurable, and integrable by [A2]. Since $0<\\delta<1$, [F5]\u2013[F6] give $\\log(1/\\delta)>0$, so $t=c\\sqrt{m\\log(1/\\delta)/2}>0$. The product law $P^m$ makes the coordinates independent by [F3], and [F7] with all $m$ coordinate bounds equal to $c$ gives $\\mathbb P\\{\\Phi-\\mathbb E\\Phi\\ge t\\}\\le\\exp(-2t^2/(mc^2))=\\delta$. By [F4], outside this event $\\Phi\\le2\\beta_m+t=2\\beta_m+(4m\\beta_m+M)\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\delta)/(2m)}$. Together with the $c=0$ case this proves the first probability bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "With $c=4\\beta_m+M/m$, the case $c=0$ again has zero losses and zero gaps. If $c>0$, [A2] makes $\\Psi$ bounded, measurable, and integrable. Since $0<\\delta<1$, [F5]\u2013[F6] give $\\log(1/\\delta)>0$. The product law $P^m$ makes the coordinates independent by [F3], and [F7] with coordinate bounds $c$ gives $\\mathbb P\\{\\Psi-\\mathbb E\\Psi\\ge c\\sqrt{m\\log(1/\\delta)/2}\\}\\le\\delta$. By [F4], $\\mathbb E\\Psi\\le\\beta_m$, so outside this event $\\Psi\\le\\beta_m+(4m\\beta_m+M)\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\delta)/(2m)}$. This proves the second probability bound, separately from the first.",
