        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-uniform-algorithmic-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal Z$ be an observation set, let $\\mathcal H$ be a set of hypotheses, and let $L:\\mathcal H\\times\\mathcal Z\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a loss. A deterministic size-indexed learning rule consists, for every integer $n\\ge1$ and every retained index set $J\\subseteq[n]$, of a map $A_{n,J}$ from the indexed observations $(z_j)_{j\\in J}$ to $\\mathcal H$; the original sample size $n$ remains an input to this rule. For $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)$ write $A_S:=A_{n,[n]}(S)$ and $A_{S\\setminus i}:=A_{n,[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}}(S|_{[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}})$ for the output after deleting indexed observation $i$. The rule is permutation-invariant if simultaneous reordering of the observations and relabeling of their retained indices leaves each output unchanged. Given a nonnegative sequence $(\\beta_n)_{n\\ge1}$, the rule has uniform deletion stability with coefficients $(\\beta_n)$ if, for every $n\\ge1$, every $S\\in\\mathcal Z^n$, every $i\\in[n]$, and every $z\\in\\mathcal Z$,\n\n$$\n|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S\\setminus i},z)|\\le\\beta_n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence is nonincreasing when $\\beta_{n+1}\\le\\beta_n$ for every $n\\ge1$. This is the deletion convention. If $S'$ differs from $S$ only at coordinate $i$, then $S\\setminus i=S'\\setminus i$, and the triangle inequality gives $|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S'},z)|\\le2\\beta_n$ for every $z$; the resulting replacement bound has coefficient $2\\beta_n$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-strongly-convex-regularized-erm-stability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathsf X,\\Sigma_X)$ and $(\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_Y)$ be nonempty measurable instance and label spaces. Let $\\kappa\\ge0$, and let $\\mathcal H$ be a real Hilbert space of $\\Sigma_X$-measurable real-valued functions on $\\mathsf X$ with kernel sections $K_x\\in\\mathcal H$ satisfying $f(x)=\\langle f,K_x\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}$ and $\\|K_x\\|_{\\mathcal H}^2=K(x,x)\\le\\kappa^2$ for every $x\\in\\mathsf X$. Let $\\lambda>0$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$, and let $c:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathsf Y\\to[0,\\infty)$ be measurable with respect to $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)\\otimes\\Sigma_Y$, convex in its first argument, and $\\sigma$-Lipschitz there, meaning $|c(u,y)-c(v,y)|\\le\\sigma|u-v|$ for all $u,v\\in\\mathbb R$ and $y\\in\\mathsf Y$. For every $n\\ge1$, every indexed sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n))$, and every retained index set $J\\subseteq[n]$, define $A_{n,J}$ as the unique attained minimizer over $f\\in\\mathcal H$ of\n\n$$\n\\frac1n\\sum_{j\\in J}c(f(x_j),y_j)+\\lambda\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal H}^2.\n$$\n\nAssume every such minimum is attained. For a size-$m$ sample, write $A_S:=A_{m,[m]}(S)$ and $A_{S\\setminus i}:=A_{m,[m]\\setminus\\{i\\}}(S|_{[m]\\setminus\\{i\\}})$, so the deleted objective retains the original denominator $m$. Then the resulting size-indexed rule is permutation-invariant and has uniform deletion stability\n\n$$\\sup_{S\\in(\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y)^m,\\ 1\\le i\\le m,\\ (x,y)\\in\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}|c(A_S(x),y)-c(A_{S\\setminus i}(x),y)|\\le\\beta_m,\\qquad\\beta_m:=\\frac{\\sigma^2\\kappa^2}{2\\lambda m}.$$\n\nFor fixed $\\lambda$, this stability envelope is nonincreasing in $m$. The conclusion supplies no uniform bounded-loss constant $M$; any such hypothesis for a concentration bound must be checked separately.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-strongly-convex-regularized-erm-stability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathsf X,\\Sigma_X)$ and $(\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_Y)$ be nonempty measurable instance and label spaces. Let $\\kappa\\ge0$, and let $\\mathcal H$ be a real Hilbert space of $\\Sigma_X$-measurable real-valued functions on $\\mathsf X$ with kernel sections $K_x\\in\\mathcal H$ satisfying $f(x)=\\langle f,K_x\\rangle_{\\mathcal H}$ and $\\|K_x\\|_{\\mathcal H}^2=K(x,x)\\le\\kappa^2$ for every $x\\in\\mathsf X$. Let $\\lambda>0$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$, and let $c:\\mathbb R\\times\\mathsf Y\\to[0,\\infty)$ be measurable with respect to $\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)\\otimes\\Sigma_Y$, convex in its first argument, and $\\sigma$-Lipschitz there, meaning $|c(u,y)-c(v,y)|\\le\\sigma|u-v|$ for all $u,v\\in\\mathbb R$ and $y\\in\\mathsf Y$. For every $n\\ge1$, every indexed sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_n,y_n))$, and every retained index set $J\\subseteq[n]$, define $A_{n,J}$ as the unique attained minimizer over $f\\in\\mathcal H$ of\n\n$$\n\\frac1n\\sum_{j\\in J}c(f(x_j),y_j)+\\lambda\\|f\\|_{\\mathcal H}^2.\n$$\n\nAssume every such minimum is attained. For a size-$m$ sample, write $A_S:=A_{m,[m]}(S)$ and $A_{S\\setminus i}:=A_{m,[m]\\setminus\\{i\\}}(S|_{[m]\\setminus\\{i\\}})$, so the deleted objective retains the original denominator $m$. Then the resulting size-indexed rule is permutation-invariant and has uniform deletion stability\n\n$$\\sup_{S\\in(\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y)^m,\\ 1\\le i\\le m,\\ (x,y)\\in\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}|c(A_S(x),y)-c(A_{S\\setminus i}(x),y)|\\le\\beta_m,\\qquad\\beta_m:=\\frac{\\sigma^2\\kappa^2}{2\\lambda m}.$$\n\nFor fixed $\\lambda$, this stability envelope is nonincreasing in $m$. The conclusion supplies no uniform bounded-loss constant $M$; any such hypothesis for a concentration bound must be checked separately.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For $t\\in[0,1]$, the objective is $p_J/m+(r_J-p_J)t/m+\\lambda t^2$, whose derivative vanishes at $(p_J-r_J)/(2\\lambda m)$; strict convexity gives the clipped formula as the unique minimizer on this interval. For $t<0$ the objective is $p_J/m-|J|t/m+\\lambda t^2$ and is decreasing toward $0$; for $t>1$ it is $|J|t/m-p_J/m+\\lambda t^2$ and is increasing away from $1$. Hence no global minimizer lies outside $[0,1]$, including when $J=\\varnothing$.",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Since $t_J,y\\in[0,1]$, $|t_J-y|\\le1$, so the common loss bound is $M=1$. Formula [F1] gives $R_P(f_t)=q(1-t)+(1-q)t$ and $\\widehat R_S(f_t)=m^{-1}[p_{[m]}(1-t)+r_{[m]}t]$ for $t\\in[0,1]$. The map $t\\mapsto|t-y|$ is convex and satisfies $||t-y|-|s-y||\\le|t-s|$, so $\\sigma=1$; the one-point RKHS has $\\kappa=1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
