    "ex-one-nearest-neighbor-has-unit-deletion-instability": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-supervised-learning-problem",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **supervised prediction problem** consists of nonempty measurable spaces $(\\mathsf X,\\Sigma_X)$ of instances, $(\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_Y)$ of labels, and $(\\mathsf A,\\Sigma_A)$ of prediction actions; the product measurable space $(\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_X\\otimes\\Sigma_Y)$; a probability measure $P$ on that product; a nonempty class $\\mathcal F$ of measurable predictors $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$; and a loss $\\ell:\\mathsf A\\times\\mathsf Y\\to[0,+\\infty]$ that is measurable with respect to $\\Sigma_A\\otimes\\Sigma_Y$. The law $P$ need not be supported on a deterministic labeling function, and this definition imposes no assumption that sample observations be independent or share a common law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a supervised prediction problem with joint law $P$, the **population risk** of any measurable predictor $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$ (in particular, every $f\\in\\mathcal F$) is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(f):=\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}\\ell(f(x),y)\\,P(d(x,y))\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor an ordered sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_m,y_m))$ of length $m\\ge1$, the **empirical risk** or **training risk** of any such $f$ is\n$$\\widehat R_S(f):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(f(x_i),y_i)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nNeither definition assumes finite risk. The sum uses extended nonnegative arithmetic; since $m\\ge1$, any summand equal to $+\\infty$ makes the empirical risk $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-uniform-algorithmic-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal Z$ be an observation set, let $\\mathcal H$ be a set of hypotheses, and let $L:\\mathcal H\\times\\mathcal Z\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a loss. A deterministic size-indexed learning rule consists, for every integer $n\\ge1$ and every retained index set $J\\subseteq[n]$, of a map $A_{n,J}$ from the indexed observations $(z_j)_{j\\in J}$ to $\\mathcal H$; the original sample size $n$ remains an input to this rule. For $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)$ write $A_S:=A_{n,[n]}(S)$ and $A_{S\\setminus i}:=A_{n,[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}}(S|_{[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}})$ for the output after deleting indexed observation $i$. The rule is permutation-invariant if simultaneous reordering of the observations and relabeling of their retained indices leaves each output unchanged. Given a nonnegative sequence $(\\beta_n)_{n\\ge1}$, the rule has uniform deletion stability with coefficients $(\\beta_n)$ if, for every $n\\ge1$, every $S\\in\\mathcal Z^n$, every $i\\in[n]$, and every $z\\in\\mathcal Z$,\n\n$$\n|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S\\setminus i},z)|\\le\\beta_n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence is nonincreasing when $\\beta_{n+1}\\le\\beta_n$ for every $n\\ge1$. This is the deletion convention. If $S'$ differs from $S$ only at coordinate $i$, then $S\\setminus i=S'\\setminus i$, and the triangle inequality gives $|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S'},z)|\\le2\\beta_n$ for every $z$; the resulting replacement bound has coefficient $2\\beta_n$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "For a fixed finite sample, nearest-index regions are finite Boolean combinations of Borel distance comparisons, so the prediction map is measurable. The tie rule depends only on the multiset of labels at the minimum distance, so reordering the training sample leaves every prediction unchanged. At feature $0$ the unique nearest label is $0$, and at feature $10$ all nearest labels are $1$; hence the rule predicts every training label correctly and [F2] gives empirical risk $0$. At feature $1$, the distances to $0$ and $10$ are $1$ and $9$, so the unique nearest label is $0$ and the loss at $(1,1)$ is $1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "After deleting the unique point $(0,0)$, every remaining training point has feature $10$ and label $1$, so the nearest-neighbor rule predicts $1$ at feature $1$ and has test loss $0$. The deletion loss change for this sample, deleted index, and test point is therefore $1$, so [F3] gives a stability coefficient at least $1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-3.1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "Every zero-one loss difference has absolute value at most $1$, so every term in the supremum [F3] is at most $1$. Step 2.1 attains this value for every $m\\ge2$, hence the coefficient is exactly $1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
