    "ex-stability-versus-training-accuracy-and-rademacher-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> ([[def-choice-function]]).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-supervised-learning-problem",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **supervised prediction problem** consists of nonempty measurable spaces $(\\mathsf X,\\Sigma_X)$ of instances, $(\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_Y)$ of labels, and $(\\mathsf A,\\Sigma_A)$ of prediction actions; the product measurable space $(\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y,\\Sigma_X\\otimes\\Sigma_Y)$; a probability measure $P$ on that product; a nonempty class $\\mathcal F$ of measurable predictors $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$; and a loss $\\ell:\\mathsf A\\times\\mathsf Y\\to[0,+\\infty]$ that is measurable with respect to $\\Sigma_A\\otimes\\Sigma_Y$. The law $P$ need not be supported on a deterministic labeling function, and this definition imposes no assumption that sample observations be independent or share a common law.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-population-and-empirical-risk",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a supervised prediction problem with joint law $P$, the **population risk** of any measurable predictor $f:\\mathsf X\\to\\mathsf A$ (in particular, every $f\\in\\mathcal F$) is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(f):=\\int_{\\mathsf X\\times\\mathsf Y}\\ell(f(x),y)\\,P(d(x,y))\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nFor an ordered sample $S=((x_1,y_1),\\ldots,(x_m,y_m))$ of length $m\\ge1$, the **empirical risk** or **training risk** of any such $f$ is\n$$\\widehat R_S(f):=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\ell(f(x_i),y_i)\\in[0,+\\infty].$$\nNeither definition assumes finite risk. The sum uses extended nonnegative arithmetic; since $m\\ge1$, any summand equal to $+\\infty$ makes the empirical risk $+\\infty$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-uniform-algorithmic-stability",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal Z$ be an observation set, let $\\mathcal H$ be a set of hypotheses, and let $L:\\mathcal H\\times\\mathcal Z\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a loss. A deterministic size-indexed learning rule consists, for every integer $n\\ge1$ and every retained index set $J\\subseteq[n]$, of a map $A_{n,J}$ from the indexed observations $(z_j)_{j\\in J}$ to $\\mathcal H$; the original sample size $n$ remains an input to this rule. For $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_n)$ write $A_S:=A_{n,[n]}(S)$ and $A_{S\\setminus i}:=A_{n,[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}}(S|_{[n]\\setminus\\{i\\}})$ for the output after deleting indexed observation $i$. The rule is permutation-invariant if simultaneous reordering of the observations and relabeling of their retained indices leaves each output unchanged. Given a nonnegative sequence $(\\beta_n)_{n\\ge1}$, the rule has uniform deletion stability with coefficients $(\\beta_n)$ if, for every $n\\ge1$, every $S\\in\\mathcal Z^n$, every $i\\in[n]$, and every $z\\in\\mathcal Z$,\n\n$$\n|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S\\setminus i},z)|\\le\\beta_n.\n$$\n\nThe sequence is nonincreasing when $\\beta_{n+1}\\le\\beta_n$ for every $n\\ge1$. This is the deletion convention. If $S'$ differs from $S$ only at coordinate $i$, then $S\\setminus i=S'\\setminus i$, and the triangle inequality gives $|L(A_S,z)-L(A_{S'},z)|\\le2\\beta_n$ for every $z$; the resulting replacement bound has coefficient $2\\beta_n$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-rademacher-complexity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of $\\mathcal A$-measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. For a fixed sample $S=(z_1,\\ldots,z_m)\\in\\mathcal Z^m$, where $m\\ge1$, let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs in $\\{-1,+1\\}$. Define\n\n$$P_mg:=\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m g(z_i),\\qquad Pg:=\\int g\\,dP,$$\n\nand define the empirical Rademacher complexity by\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\left[\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(z_i)\\right].$$\n\nHere $\\mathbb E_\\sigma$ is the uniform average over the $2^m$ sign vectors. If $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ is an IID sample with common law $P$, and the displayed supremum is measurable as a function of $S$, the expected Rademacher complexity is\n\n$$\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G).$$\n\nThe class may be finite, countable, or uncountable when the indicated supremum is measurable; a specified countable subclass giving the same supremum for every sign vector for almost every sample suffices. This is the one-sided convention: the definition contains no absolute value, and zero need not belong to $\\mathcal G$. Both complexities lie in $[0,1]$: every signed average lies in $[-1,1]$, and for any fixed $g_0\\in\\mathcal G$ the sign-average supremum is at least the sign-average for $g_0$, which is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-rademacher-uniform-generalization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice (AC). Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\mathcal A,P)$ be a probability space, let $m\\ge1$, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a nonempty class of measurable functions $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,1]$. Take $S=(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_m)$ to be the coordinate sample with law $P^m$. Put $P_mg=m^{-1}\\sum_i g(Z_i)$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}:=\\{1-g:g\\in\\mathcal G\\}$. Assume the sample, independent-copy and sign suprema over $\\mathcal G$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal G}$ used in the displayed claims and the symmetrization argument are measurable. Let $\\sigma_1,\\ldots,\\sigma_m$ be independent uniform signs, and let\n\n$$\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_\\sigma\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sigma_i g(Z_i),\\qquad \\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G):=\\mathbb E_{S\\sim P^m}\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G),$$\n\nwhere the $\\sigma_i$ are independent uniform signs, and let $\\delta\\in(0,1)$. Each of the following four guarantees holds with probability at least $1-\\delta$ over $S$:\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(1/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\forall g\\in\\mathcal G,\\qquad Pg\\le P_mg+2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\mathfrak R_m(\\mathcal G)+\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{2m}},$$\n\n$$\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}|Pg-P_mg|\\le2\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)+3\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(4/\\delta)}{2m}}.$$\n\nThe four guarantees are separate probability statements. The choice axiom is used through the bounded-differences theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-natural-logarithm",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For $x>0$, define $\\log x$ to be the unique real $y$ such that $\\exp(y)=x$. This is well-defined because [[cor-exponential-is-a-bijection-onto-positive-reals]] states that $\\exp:\\mathbb R\\to(0,\\infty)$ is a bijection.\n\nThus $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is the inverse function of $\\exp$. In particular, $\\log(\\exp y)=y$ for every $y\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\exp(\\log x)=x$ for every $x>0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-natural-logarithm-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The function $\\log:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ is continuous and strictly increasing, is onto $\\mathbb R$, and satisfies, for $x,y>0$,\n$$\\log(xy)=\\log x+\\log y,\\qquad \\log(x/y)=\\log x-\\log y,\\qquad \\log(1/x)=-\\log x.$$\nAlso $\\log 1=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Both rules depend only on the sample multiset, so they are permutation-invariant. Under $P$, $h_0\\equiv0$ has zero loss at every feature, so its population and empirical risks are both $0$. For $S=((j,0))$, the memorizing learner predicts $0$ at $j$ and $1$ at the other $N-1$ features. Thus its empirical risk is $0$ and its population risk is $(N-1)/N$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-1.2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "Fix any $z=(j,y)$ and one-point sample $S=(z)$. Since $\\mathcal H$ contains every binary labeling, some $h$ has loss $g_h(z)=0$ and some has $g_h(z)=1$. For sign $\\sigma=+1$, $\\sup_{g\\in\\mathcal G}\\sigma g(z)=1$; for sign $\\sigma=-1$, the supremum is $0$. The two equiprobable signs therefore give $\\widehat{\\mathfrak R}_S(\\mathcal G)=1/2$ for every $S$, and averaging over $S\\sim P$ gives $\\mathfrak R_1(\\mathcal G)=1/2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derivation-2.1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "The constant learner returns $h_0$ on both $S$ and its deletion, so its size-one stability coefficient is $0$. The empty-sample memorizer predicts $1$ at $j$, whereas the full-sample memorizer predicts $0$ there; at test point $(j,0)$ the loss changes by $1$. Every zero-one loss difference is at most $1$, hence the memorizer's coefficient is exactly $1$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
